+# GHSA-mwv6-3258-q52c (CVE-2025-55184) — False Positive / Already Addressed for next@13.5.4
+
+## Summary
+
+**GHSA-mwv6-3258-q52c does not require a separate security patch on next@13.5.4.**
+The vulnerability is the original React Server Components cyclic-thenable DoS
+(CVE-2025-55184). The incomplete-fix follow-up **GHSA-5j59-xgg2-r9c4
+(CVE-2025-67779)** ships the complete cycle-protection counter (`cycleProtection > 1000`)
+in `Chunk.prototype.then`. That follow-up patch fully covers this advisory.
+
+## Advisory
+
+- **GHSA:** GHSA-mwv6-3258-q52c
+- **CVE:** CVE-2025-55184
+- **Package:** next@13.5.4
+- **Description:** Crafted HTTP requests to App Router Server Function endpoints can
+  cause the RSC deserializer to hang / consume CPU (cyclic thenables).
+- **Upstream affected range:** `>= 13.3.0, < 14.2.34` (and later 15.x/16.x lines)
+- **Upstream patched versions:** 14.2.34, 15.0.6, …
+- **Follow-up advisory:** GHSA-5j59-xgg2-r9c4 / CVE-2025-67779 (incomplete-fix follow-up)
+
+## Why this version is covered without a second patch
+
+next@13.5.4 is in the affected range. The same vendored
+`dist/compiled/react-server-dom-*` `Chunk.prototype.then` INITIALIZED path is
+hardened by the GHSA-5j59-xgg2-r9c4 patch applied to this package version.
+Applying the original (incomplete) CVE-2025-55184 change as a second patch would
+conflict with that follow-up on the same hunks.
+
+## References
+
+- https://github.com/vercel/next.js/security/advisories/GHSA-mwv6-3258-q52c
+- https://github.com/vercel/next.js/security/advisories/GHSA-5j59-xgg2-r9c4
+- https://nextjs.org/blog/security-update-2025-12-11
+- https://www.cve.org/CVERecord?id=CVE-2025-55184

Description: Fix GHSA-q4gf-8mx6-v5v3 (CVE-2026-23869) - bound RSC deserialization work to prevent CPU DoS
 Adds a string-literal-aware O(n) scan (enforceModelStringLimits) over the raw
 JSON model text before initializeModelChunk hands it to JSON.parse, capping
 structural (object/array) nesting depth at 1000 by default and, when an
 explicit arraySizeLimit is supplied via decodeReply's new options parameter,
 also bounding the cumulative structural-node count across chunks. Prevents an
 attacker-supplied Server Actions/flight request body with deeply nested
 arrays/objects from causing unbounded CPU consumption during decodeReply.
 .
 next@13.5.4 inlines its own copy of this same react-server-dom-webpack
 model-chunk-resolution logic into the pre-bundled runtimes it actually
 requires at request time (dist/compiled/next-server/app-page*.runtime.*.js -
 dev/prod, default and experimental, and the turbo variants). The standalone
 dist/compiled/react-server-dom-webpack/cjs/*.js files patched above are NOT
 loaded by `next start`/`next dev` for App Router Server Actions, so a depth
 guard there alone does not protect a running app. This revision adds a
 minimal inline depth-cap (same default-1000 check, no arraySizeLimit/node-
 count plumbing since these internal call sites never pass decodeReply
 options) directly before every JSON.parse(chunk.value, chunk._response.
 _fromJSON) call site inside those six next-server runtime bundles, verified
 by an end-to-end `next build && next start` HTTP request that previously hit
 unguarded parsing and now fails fast with "Maximum array nesting exceeded".
